From 362b19aab8e0413bc1f2ac956021f82ceb0df91c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 16:39:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count what phase 3 is actually aimed at, item 3-8
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

3-6 specified this XL from one sentence — "the dropped candidates are dropped
for want of a type, not for want of a rule" — and 3-7 made it the whole of
what phase 3 has left. Counted before building any of it, the item is
well-founded mechanically and aimed at almost none of the prize.

Two numbers, neither previously taken.

NumberBoxingDiagnostics counts every JSNumber.Create, split by whether the
small-integer table answers it. Number boxing is 41.89% of everything the
Octane corpus allocates -- 2.05 GB of 4.88 GB -- and the per-suite spread is
what hid it: 66.96% of NavierStokes, 55.16% of Crypto, 35.98% of Box2D against
0.31% of DeltaBlue. So the prize was always large.

BROILER_JS_NUMERIC_LOCALS=0 removes the numeric-local tier entirely, which is
the control four phase 3 items were each measured without. The whole tier --
P2-2 item 3, 3-0, 3-3, 3-5 and 3-7 together -- removes 311 187 boxes of
85.6 M, 0.36%, and 0.41% of allocation; six interleaved driver runs an arm do
not separate on wall clock at all (21 261 vs 21 024 ms, ranges overlapping end
to end). Four "invisible on the corpus" readings were never evidence about the
mechanism; they were evidence that eight more names do not matter.

A box is minted by the operator, not by the local. The drop-cause classifier
says the same from the other side: of 1 916 drops, 894 (46.7%) take their
value from a property read and 570 (29.7%) from a call -- 76.4% produced
elsewhere -- against 47 (2.5%) from a parameter, the category 3-3 deferred to
phase 4 as the one that mattered. 93.1% are roots rather than cascades.

So 3-8 as written should not be started, and 3-1 and 3-2 move ahead of it:
checked in their sources, NavierStokes and Crypto hold their numbers in
new Array and read them by index with no field access at all, while Box2D
allocates no arrays and has 240 .x/.y accesses.

Also fixes a bookkeeping defect found writing the classifier's tests: the
Collector descends into nested functions on purpose, but VisitBlock was also
offering the blocks it met there, so a nested function's block-scoped var
became a candidate of every enclosing function and was dropped and counted
once per level. No answer changes -- the enclosing function's hoisting scope
never contains the name -- and dropped, surviving, numericLocals, hoistedNames
and every drop cause come back identical; item 3-7's offered/rejected pair is
corrected from 2 521/359 to 2 295/133.

Adds NumericDropCauseTests (14 cases). 7 910 tests pass.
